00:53:46.990 --> 00:53:53.140
<v Ira Glass>You thought that the pants had actually done a job on him? You thought the pants were part of your arsenal and part of your power over him?
<v Ira Glass>它们是——是的，军火库。正是。它们给了我自信，我认为这给了我对这个我最终想要吸引的人的一定程度的吸引力。因为显然，当我穿着那条裤子走来走去时，我感到非常自信。我觉得我看起来棒极了。

00:53:53.140 --> 00:54:07.960
<v Kate Porterfield>They were-- yeah, arsenal. Exactly. They gave me confidence, which gave me some, I think, some degree maybe of attractiveness to this person who ultimately I was trying to attract. Because clearly, when I was walking around in those pants, I was feeling pretty confident. I thought I looked great.
<v Kate Porterfield>你觉得如果没有裤子你也能得到乔尔吗？

00:54:07.960 --> 00:54:10.990
<v Ira Glass>Do you think you could have gotten Joel without the pants?
<v Ira Glass>哇。天哪。我甚至都不愿去想它，艾拉。我不知道。

00:54:10.990 --> 00:54:17.120
<v Kate Porterfield>Wow. Gosh. I hate to even have to think about it, Ira. I don't know.
<v Kate Porterfield>你觉得裤子是那么大的一个因素吗？

00:54:17.120 --> 00:54:18.340
<v Ira Glass>You think the pants were that big of a factor?
<v Ira Glass>我不知道！我不知道。我的意思是，那已经是11年前的事了，但也许那条裤子和它有点关系。你知道，我得问他。我得问他。

00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:28.240
<v Kate Porterfield>I don't know! I don't know. I mean, it's been 11 years, but maybe those pants had something to do with it. You know, I'd have have to ask him. I'd have to ask him.
<v Kate Porterfield>如果她每天穿着廉价童装或休闲西装什么的出现，我也会爱上她的。我的意思是，正如任何恋爱过的人都知道的那样，你偶像化你爱上的人。特别是在初期，他们对你来说感觉是完美的。然后你开始问自己各种各样的问题。各种各样的不安全感涌上心头。为什么这个完美的人一定会爱上我，并接受我，想要和我在一起？

00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:56.470
<v Joel Lovell>I would have fallen for her if she were showing up in Garanimals every day, say, or leisure suits or something. I mean, as anybody knows who's ever fallen in love, you idolize the person you're falling in love with. And they can, in those early days particularly, they feel perfect to you. And then you start asking yourself all sorts of questions. All sorts of insecurities come up. Why would this perfect person necessarily fall for me, and accept me and want to be with me?
<v Joel Lovell>但当你发现那个人完美中的一道裂痕，当你发现一个缺陷——在这个例子里，凯特至少在那条裤子上有糟糕的品味——它不知怎么地让你觉得更舒服了一点。你觉得，好吧，也许我确实有机会，因为，天哪，我的意思是那条裤子都有机会追到她。

00:54:56.470 --> 00:55:22.980
<v Joel Lovell>But then when you discover a chink in that person's perfection, when you find a flaw-- in this case Kate had bad taste at least in this one pair of pants-- then it somehow makes you feel a little bit more comfortable. You feel like, well, maybe I do have a chance here because, God, I mean those pants had a chance with her.
<v Joel Lovell>乔尔·洛弗尔和凯特·波特菲尔德。自从那个故事2001年在我们的节目中首次播出以来，他们的家庭已经增加到三个孩子、两个大人，没有酸洗牛仔裤。

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:33.200
<v Ira Glass>Joel Lovell and Kate Porterfield. Since that story originally broadcast on our program in 2001, their family has grown to three kids, two adults, no acid-washed jeans.
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 吉米·克兰顿的《VENUS IN BLUE JEANS》]

